# font metadata

## metrics

meta fixed-pitch // not exactly true, but typical for dual-width terminal fonts
meta ascent 13
meta descent 3
meta cap-height 10
meta x-height 8 // derive from `x-lower`
meta underline-at -2 1
meta strikeout-at 1 4

## strings

meta designer `Kang Seonghoon`
meta copyright `The author of Unison, Kang Seonghoon, disclaims copyright to the font data. For jurisdictions where authors cannot disclaim their copyright, it is distributed under CC0 1.0 Universal.`
meta license `Public domain where the author can disclaim copyright; otherwise CC0 1.0 Universal.`
meta license-url https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/

## faces and slices

face regular : wide
meta regular : family Unison
meta regular : subfamily Regular

face term : narrow
meta term : family `Unison Term`
meta term : subfamily Regular

// being a monospace font, Unison has to deal with East Asian Width,
// a category that determines whether the character is half-width, full-width or ambiguous.
// the handling of EAW=Ambiguous is especially annoying because
// CJK and non-CJK environments generally expect different behaviors.
// these slices essentially represent each environment.
slice narrow // EAW=Ambiguous is narrow (typical in non-CJK, for terminals)
slice wide // EAW=Ambiguous is wide (typical in CJK)

// by convention, most environment-dependent glyphs are named so that
// the narrow version is named after the wide version plus `-half`.
// this special name-parts definition simplifies this naming convention.
name-parts wide : $-half = ``
name-parts narrow : $-half = -half

